Explanation:
Step-by-Step Solution:
1. Statutory Text: RA 8749, Section 14:
 'Air Quality Management Fund. — An Air Quality Management Fund to be administered by the Department as a special account in the National Treasury is hereby established. It shall be financed from:
 (a) Emission charges and fees;
 (b) Fines and penalties imposed for violations of this Act;
 (c) Grants, donations, endowments and contributions...
 THE FUND SHALL BE USED EXCLUSIVELY TO FINANCE THE FOLLOWING:
 (1) Research, development, and monitoring programs on air quality;
 (2) Procurement of automated air monitoring and laboratory equipment;
 (3) Capability-building and training of regulatory and enforcement personnel;
 (4) Supporting the operations of Airshed Governing Boards.'
2. Fiduciary Safeguard: Section 14 explicitly prohibits diverting AQMF funds to general government spending or personal expenses.
3. Option A is the verbatim statutory mandate and purpose.

Common Trap: Believing environmental fines go into the general fund; Clean Air Act fines go directly into the AIR QUALITY MANAGEMENT FUND (AQMF) to fund air monitoring.
Exam Tip: Air Quality Management Fund (RA 8749, Sec. 14) = Special Trust Fund financed by emission fees and penalties, earmarked EXCLUSIVELY for air monitoring, clean research, and enforcement.
